<<~ ahu slot=`#/invitation`>>
! Example Six ~ artistic lettering with exact output obligations

```tiddlywiki
<<art worktype=`minimal typographic poster` subject=`a short instruction to notice the present moment` read=`a quiet invitation, not an advertisement` imagery=`one large line of lettering above ample visual rest` treatment=`warm restrained ink impression with subtle letterpress-like edge character` composition=`one asymmetrically balanced line with generous margins` relation=`the text and unprinted space establish a deliberate pause` depiction-scope=`only the poster; no maker, photographed office, or metadata labels`>>
```
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/exact-text`>>
The Art Sigil governs what the typography should do aesthetically. The separate artifact contract governs whether the exact required characters have been delivered:

```toml
[artifact]
role = "typographic poster"
exact_text = "NOTICE THE PRESENT."
spelling_and_punctuation = "mandatory"
layout = "line count and margins subject to artistic review"
validation = "inspect visible letters and final export; deterministic typesetting may be required"
```

The receiver must not infer that an apparently legible generated string satisfies a literal text requirement. Exactness may be established by typesetting directly or by a verified downstream text plate. Merely describing a letterpress impression does not prove that a physical letterpress was used.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/noninheritance`>>
Mandatory exact lettering is an artifact-specific requirement, not a universal need for printed text or a new ninth dimension of `art()`.
<<~/ahu>>

